.TH LUA 1 "" "" "mcos Manual"
.SH NAME
lua \- Lua interpreter
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lua
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ script
.RI [ args ...]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lua
is Lua's standalone interpreter, as
.I lua.c
is. It runs Lua given as text: statements from
.BR \-e ,
a
.I script
file, standard input, or lines typed at its interactive prompt (the
REPL).
.PP
Every mcos program is already Lua: the kernel starts each process through
the loader,
.IR /lib/ld.lua ,
which opens the standard library and runs the program, so programs do not
need
.B lua
to run.
.B lua
is a program like any other.
.PP
Options are handled in order:
.B \-e
and
.B \-l
run as they are met, then the
.I script
runs with
.I args
as its
.B ...
and in the global table
.BR arg :
.B arg[0]
is the script,
.B arg[1]
on its arguments, and the interpreter and its options at negative indices.
What the script returns is ignored.
.PP
With no script and no
.B \-e
or
.BR \-v ,
.B lua
starts the REPL if standard input is a terminal, and otherwise runs
standard input as a script.
.PP
An error is reported on standard error as
.BI "lua: " message
followed by a stack traceback (the interpreter's own frames left out).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-e " stat"
Run the statement
.IR stat .
.TP
.BI \-l " mod"
Require module
.I mod
into the global
.IR mod .
.TP
.BI \-l " g" = mod
Require module
.I mod
into the global
.IR g .
.TP
.B \-i
Enter the REPL after running
.I script
(and print the version first).
.TP
.B \-v
Print the version.
.TP
.B "\-E \-W"
Accepted, and do nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-
Stop handling options.
.TP
.B \-
Stop handling options and run standard input.
.PP
The argument of
.B \-e
and
.B \-l
may also be joined to it
.RB ( \-lfs ).
.SH THE REPL
The prompt is
.BR "> " ;
a line that is an incomplete statement goes on at
.BR ">> " .
A line is first tried as an expression; its values are printed,
separated by tabs, a table as its contents (readably, through
.IR /lib/serialization.lua ),
other values as
.B print
shows them.
.PP
Lines are read with
.I /lib/readline.lua
and its keys:
.TP
.B "Left Right Home End ^A ^E"
Move the cursor.
.TP
.B "Backspace Delete ^D"
Erase the character before or under the cursor.
.TP
.B "^U ^K ^W"
Cut the line before the cursor, after it, or the word before it.
.TP
.B "Up Down"
Go through the history (100 lines).
.TP
.B ^L
Clear the screen.
.TP
.B Tab
Complete a Lua name: a global, or a field of the table named before the
last
.B .
or
.B :
(methods through
.B __index
too). A table's name gets a
.BR . ,
a function's a
.BR ( .
A second Tab lists the choices.
.TP
.B ^C
Drop the line being typed; while code runs, end it with
.B interrupted!
at its next system call (code that makes none runs on).
.TP
.B ^D
On an empty line, leave the REPL.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 on a bad option, or if a chunk given by
.BR \-e ,
.BR \-l ,
the script or standard input fails to load or raises an error.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
lua \-e "print(2^10)"
lua \-l fs \-e 'print(fs.isdir("/tmp"))'
lua script.lua one two
echo "print(1+1)" | lua
.fi
.SH NOTES
.B \-E
and
.B \-W
have no effect: there are no
.B LUA_INIT
variables and warnings go nowhere.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sh (1),
.BR edit (1)
